**Read:** [Physical Geology, second edition — Steven Earle](../sources/Physical-Geology-Earle.lingua#r_5GWEYVQGR176TXMQT0S881ZSKX), chapter 8’s relative-dating treatment; [Introduction to Historical Geology — Estrada and contributing authors](../sources/Introduction-to-Historical-Geology.lingua#r_1JKSSECEDW0M9QG30TJ1D9KHZD), Sequences of Strata and the complete relative-time material; [Laboratory Manual for Introductory Geology — Deline, Harris, and Tefend](../sources/Introductory-Geology-Laboratory-Manual.lingua#r_7G68RZ432EQWTB84DSVJ4JCZ2Z), the complete relative-dating exercises.

Relative chronology uses relationships such as superposition, cross-cutting, inclusions, intrusive contacts, unconformities, and deformation overprints under their stated conditions. Explain why a relationship constrains an order and what could complicate it. A description of tilted or overturned strata needs structural context before a simple top-to-bottom sequence is accepted.

Distinguish deposition, crystallization, intrusion, deformation, metamorphism, erosion, weathering, exposure, and measurement as events. A rock can contain inherited material older than deposition and minerals younger than its original formation. “The age of the rock” is incomplete until the event and evidence are specified.

A partial order is often more honest than one fully specified story. Some relationships establish that one event preceded another while leaving their duration or order relative to a third unresolved. A gap in exposure or record can preserve several alternative histories. Later numerical constraints should be attached to the particular events they date.

**Work:** interpret an authorized textbook outcrop diagram or image, listing observed relationships and a justified event order. Construct two histories consistent with the same incomplete evidence and identify a discriminating observation. Add a hypothetical age constraint and explain which event interval it bounds.

Read the complete relative-time sections and apply them to actual described field relations. Historical reasoning and physical/chemical mechanisms work together; a plausible mechanism alone does not establish that it occurred in that order at that place.

